# submit_pipeline.conf.example — template for submit_pipeline.conf.
#
# submit_pipeline.sh refuses to run unless submit_pipeline.conf exists next
# to it (same directory). Copy this file to submit_pipeline.conf and edit
# the values below for your setup:
#
#   cp submit_pipeline.conf.example submit_pipeline.conf
#
# submit_pipeline.conf is gitignored — it holds your own local paths and
# should not be committed. This file (the .example) is the tracked template.
#
# Every value here is just a default: any of submit_pipeline.sh's flags
# (--input-script, --rdf-r-max, --nodes, ...) overrides the corresponding
# variable below for that one invocation. Run submit_pipeline.sh --help for
# what each variable/flag controls.
#
# This file is sourced as bash, so normal bash quoting rules apply and two
# variables submit_pipeline.sh sets before sourcing it are available:
#
#   $REPO_ROOT  the util checkout (default $HOME/util)
#   $RUN_DIR    the LAMMPS run directory the pipeline was invoked from (the cwd).
#               Writing a value as "$RUN_DIR/..." ties it to whichever run you
#               are submitting instead of to one hardcoded run — the input script
#               and the overwrite log below both work that way, so this file
#               needs no editing when you move to run 0169.
#   $RUN_ID     that directory's basename (e.g. 0168). Also the prefix of the
#               stage-2 output directory, <run_id>_distribution_analysis.
#
# They describe where you ran the script, so they are fixed before any flag is
# parsed. A third, $PARENT_DIR, is defined by this file rather than by the
# script — see the general input parameters below.

# =============================================================================
# pipeline behaviour — what RUNS, as opposed to what it computes
# =============================================================================
# Each of these has a submit_pipeline.sh flag of the same name (--skip-trajectory,
# --interactive, --force REASON, --clean), and the flag wins for that invocation.
# They are per-run decisions far more often than they are settings, so the
# resting state for all five is the value shown here. Leaving any of them out of
# this file is fine — the script falls back to exactly these defaults.
#
#   SKIP_TRAJECTORY  1 = skip stage 1 (the LAMMPS run) and analyse the trajectory
#                    already sitting in run/. This is the normal way to add a
#                    calculation to a finished run. Aborts up front if the dump
#                    files it expects are missing.
#   INTERACTIVE      1 = run both stages in the foreground rather than through
#                    sbatch. Needs an allocation you already hold (salloc); it
#                    does not request one.
#   FORCE            1 = overwrite stage 1's input_files/ and run/, AND redo
#                    stage-2 calculations whose output is already in the analysis
#                    directory. Requires FORCE_REASON. Setting this to 1 here
#                    disarms the guard on every run — prefer the flag, which
#                    disarms it once.
#   FORCE_REASON     Why you are overwriting. Logged with a timestamp and the
#                    affected paths to LOG_FILE below. FORCE=1 with an empty
#                    reason aborts rather than guessing.
#   CLEAN            1 = delete the whole <run_id>_distribution_analysis/ before
#                    stage 2. Off by default: an existing analysis directory is
#                    ADDED TO, so a run that computes only vdos leaves an earlier
#                    rdf in place, and only a REPEATED calculation needs FORCE.
SKIP_TRAJECTORY="0"
INTERACTIVE="0"
FORCE="0"
FORCE_REASON=""
CLEAN="0"

# =============================================================================
# pipeline paths and trajectory filenames
# =============================================================================
# Change these only if your in.input writes its dumps under other names, or you
# keep the slurm template somewhere else. $REPO_ROOT is available here.
#
#   LAMMPS_TEMPLATE     sbatch template copied in as stage 1's job script
#   DUMP_FILE           structural trajectory, read by rdf_freud.py/bad_freud.py
#   DYNAMICS_DUMP_FILE  dynamics trajectory, read by dsf.py/vdos.py/msd.py
#   LOG_FILE            append-only record of every FORCE overwrite and CLEAN
#                       deletion, with timestamps and the exact paths. Kept in
#                       the run directory, next to the data it describes, so the
#                       record of what was overwritten travels with the run.
LAMMPS_TEMPLATE="$REPO_ROOT/jobs/slurm/lammps_submit.slurm"
DUMP_FILE="dump.lammpstrj"
DYNAMICS_DUMP_FILE="dynamics.lammpstrj"
LOG_FILE="$RUN_DIR/overwrite.log"

# =============================================================================
# general input parameters
# =============================================================================
# PARENT_DIR is the campaign this run belongs to — one directory holding the
# potentials being tested, the runs that use them, and the analysis of those
# runs. It is not read by submit_pipeline.sh; it exists so the two values under
# it are written once, here, and every run in the campaign inherits them:
#
#   $PARENT_DIR/potentials/<name>   the potential file
#   $PARENT_DIR/analysis            where <run_id>_distribution_analysis/ is
#                                   created (the script mkdir -p's it)
#   $PARENT_DIR/runs/<run_id>       the run directories, i.e. where you cd to
#                                   before invoking the pipeline — which is what
#                                   the script hands back as $RUN_DIR
#
# Point PARENT_DIR at a new campaign and the potential and analysis locations
# follow; nothing else in this file has to change. INPUT_SCRIPT is written
# against $RUN_DIR instead, because the .input belongs to the individual run
# rather than to the campaign.
PARENT_DIR="/scratch1/lkyamamo/test-runs/potential-verify"

INPUT_SCRIPT="$RUN_DIR/OH-therm.input"
STARTING_STRUCTURE="$REPO_ROOT/starting-structures/ICE_CUBIC.data"
POTENTIAL_FILE="$PARENT_DIR/potentials/20260723_OH.vashishta"
POTENTIAL_LINK_NAME="OH.usc"
ANALYSIS_PARENT_DIR="$PARENT_DIR/analysis"
ANALYSIS_TEMPLATE_DIR="$REPO_ROOT/analysis/distributions/20260608_GrNrBaSqw"
RUN_DSF="0"
RUN_RDF="1"
RUN_BAD="1"
RUN_VDOS="0"
RUN_MSD="0"
RUN_VDOS_DYNMAT="0"

# LAMMPS executable. Empty = the default in jobs/slurm/lammps_submit.slurm,
# which is .../lmp_mpi_phonon_2019 — the phonon build, since dynamical_matrix
# needs the PHONON package. Point this at .../lmp_mpi_shock_2019 to go back to
# the previous binary.
LMP_BIN=""

# trajectory creation slurm parameters
NODES="1"
NTASKS="128"
TIME="1:00:00"
JOB_NAME="water-setup"
CONSTRAINT="epyc-9554"
NODELIST=""

# analysis slurm parameters
ANALYSIS_NODES="1"
ANALYSIS_NTASKS="1"
ANALYSIS_TIME="1:00:00"
ANALYSIS_JOB_NAME="water-distributions"
ANALYSIS_CONSTRAINT="epyc-9554"
ANALYSIS_CPUS_PER_TASK=""
ANALYSIS_NODELIST=""

# =============================================================================
# distribution code parameters
# =============================================================================
# Every key below is a default that the matching submit_pipeline.sh flag can
# override for one invocation. Empty = leave the script's own default in effect
# (see the CONFIGURATION block at the top of each .py). Keys marked REQUIRED
# have no default and abort the run up front rather than guessing.
#
# Each block states how to CHOOSE its values, not just what they mean. Worked
# examples assume the reference system used throughout: liquid water /
# hydroxylated silica, ~5000 atoms, cubic cell L = 43 A, MD timestep 0.5 fs.
#
# SAMPLING: is it converged?
# --------------------------
# Every analysis reports, at the end of its run, the number of independent
# contributions M behind its noisiest output, and the relative error that
# implies:
#     relative error ~ 1/sqrt(M)
#     M = 1e2   10%    exploratory only
#     M = 1e3    3%    PASS MARK - peak positions, coordination numbers
#     M = 1e4    1%    publication / comparison against measured data
#     M = 1e6  0.1%    past the point of diminishing returns
# M is (contributors per configuration) x (independent configurations), so it is
# size-invariant: double the atom count and you can halve the frames. Raise it by
# adding frames or atoms - both enter linearly. Each run names its LIMITING
# partial or species, which is normally the rarest one and is invisible in any
# aggregate.
#
# Two ways M lies, pointing to opposite advice:
#   vdos.py/msd.py  Overlapping VACF windows are not independent. With
#                   CORR_LENGTH=2000 and CORR_INTERVAL=100 consecutive origins
#                   share 95% of the same data. Independent windows are capped at
#                   span/CORR_LENGTH - a ceiling shrinking CORR_INTERVAL cannot
#                   raise, though it does keep costing runtime. Both scripts print
#                   M_raw and M_indep so the gap is visible.
#   rdf/bad/dsf     Closely spaced frames are correlated, but the many pairs
#                   within ONE frame are not, which is why g(r) converges fast.
#                   Prefer fewer, well-separated frames over many adjacent ones.
#
# COST: will it finish?
# ---------------------
# Measured on a 12-core mac (134784-atom frame for rdf, 5184 atoms x 10 frames
# for bad/dsf). Publish-worthy exponents, not textbook ones:
#     rdf_freud.py  ~ frames x N x R_MAX^3   8.2x per R_MAX doubling (8->16 A)
#     bad_freud.py  ~ R_CUTOFF^6 (!)         4->6 A measured 16x. Coordination
#                                            grows as R^3 and triplets as its
#                                            square, so a "safe" generous cutoff
#                                            is quadratically worse than it looks
#     dsf.py        ~ frames x N x Q_MAX^3   Q_MAX=20 costs ~10 min on only
#                                            5184 atoms x 10 frames - by far the
#                                            most expensive default here; drop to
#                                            12 while iterating (4.6x cheaper)
#     vdos/msd      ~ n_refs x CORR_LENGTH x N, linear in 1/CORR_INTERVAL
#     vdos_dynmat   ~ (3N)^3 time, (3N)^2 memory
#
# THREADS: how many are worth asking for?
# ---------------------------------------
# Not all backends read OMP_NUM_THREADS - freud and numba ignore it, so the
# scripts and runners now set freud.parallel.set_num_threads() and
# NUMBA_NUM_THREADS explicitly. Measured for rdf_freud.py (134784 atoms,
# R_MAX=6), wall / core-seconds:
#     1 thread 2.01 s / 2.0     4 threads 0.76 s / 3.0  <- efficient range
#     2 threads 1.19 s / 2.4    12 threads 0.61 s / 7.3
# Parallel efficiency is 66% at 4 threads and 28% at 12, so 12 costs 3.6x the
# CPU for 3.3x the speed; asking for 64 wastes the allocation. Note that MORE
# FRAMES does not improve this - each frame is its own compute() call, so frames
# add work at unchanged efficiency. Only more atoms or a larger R_MAX (to ~8) do.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# rdf_freud.py — g(r) and coordination numbers. Reads dump.lammpstrj.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   RDF_R_MAX          How far out to compute g(r), in A. HARD LIMIT: must be
#                      < half the shortest box edge, or freud cannot minimum-
#                      image the pair distances and the script aborts. At
#                      L = 43 A that ceiling is 21.4 A. Use 8 A for the first
#                      few coordination shells (peak positions, coordination
#                      numbers); use 20 A if the g(r) will be Fourier
#                      transformed, or if you care that it flattens to 1.
#   RDF_BINS_VAL       Bin count. Width = R_MAX / BINS; 0.01-0.02 A resolves
#                      the first peak well. Finer is not free — with few frames
#                      it just adds noise. 8 A / 800 = 0.010 A, as does
#                      20 A / 2000.
#   RDF_NORMALIZATION_VAL  Pair-weight convention(s), SEMICOLON-separated (a
#                      comma would be truncated by sbatch --export):
#                        unity     b = 1, mole fractions only
#                        FZ        Faber-Ziman, divided by <b>^2
#                        absolute  unnormalized, barn/sr/atom
#                      Choosing: comparing to a published FZ-normalized curve
#                      -> FZ; to a measured differential cross-section on an
#                      absolute scale -> absolute; want a b-free structural
#                      summary -> unity. Unsure? Leave empty and get all three.
#   RDF_FUNCTIONS_VAL  Correlation function(s), SEMICOLON-separated, from
#                      g | h | D | T. The cross product with the conventions
#                      above is emitted, one column per combination named
#                      <function>_<convention>. h with absolute is Soper's
#                      neutron G_n(r); D with FZ is the PDF community's G(r).
#                      Empty leaves the script defaults, "FZ;absolute;unity"
#                      and "g;h;D".
#   RDF_RESOLUTION_SIGMA_VAL  Gaussian resolution broadening in A; 0 disables
#                      (script default 0.1). Match it to the instrument you are
#                      comparing against — Soper used ~0.1 A. Without it,
#                      modelled peaks are sharper than measured ones for purely
#                      instrumental reasons. The unbroadened curve is always
#                      kept alongside as a *_broadened twin.
RDF_R_MAX="8"
RDF_BINS_VAL="800"
RDF_NORMALIZATION_VAL=""
RDF_FUNCTIONS_VAL=""
RDF_RESOLUTION_SIGMA_VAL=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# bad_freud.py — bond angle distributions. Reads dump.lammpstrj.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   BAD_ELEMENTS       SEMICOLON-separated element list; the script never
#                      auto-detects. Must name every element you want angles
#                      for, e.g. "Si;O;H" or "O;H".
#   BAD_R_CUTOFF       Upper neighbour cutoff per pair — which neighbours count
#                      as bonded. HOW TO CHOOSE: run rdf_freud.py FIRST and read
#                      the first minimum of that pair's g(r). Guessing here
#                      silently changes every angle distribution downstream.
#                      Format "El1-El2:value;...", keys sorted alphabetically.
#                      Typical water/silica first minima: O-H 1.2-1.4 A
#                      (covalent), H-O 2.4 A (H-bond), O-O 3.2-3.5 A, Si-O 2.2 A.
#   BAD_R_MINCUT       Lower cutoff per pair, same format; excludes unphysical
#                      close contacts. The rising edge of the first peak, or
#                      ~0.5 A, is the usual choice.
#   BAD_TRIPLET_CUTOFFS  Per-triplet overrides, pipe-separated entries of
#                      label:elA-elB-elC:r_max_ab:r_min_ab:r_max_cb:r_min_cb.
#                      This is how you separate covalent from hydrogen-bonded
#                      versions of the same angle — the default below does
#                      exactly that for water.
#   BAD_BINS_VAL       Bins over 0-180 deg. 180 = 1 deg, 360 = 0.5 deg.
BAD_ELEMENTS="O;H"
BAD_R_CUTOFF=""
BAD_R_MINCUT=""
BAD_TRIPLET_CUTOFFS="H-O-H:H-O-H:1.2:0.5:1.2:0.5|H--O--H:H-O-H:2.4:1.49:2.4:1.49|O-H--O:O-H-O:1.2:0.5:2.4:1.49"
BAD_BINS_VAL="360"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# dsf.py — static S(q) and dynamic S(q,w). Reads dynamics.lammpstrj.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every dsf.py key is DSF_-prefixed, like VDOS_* and MSD_*, with one exception:
# the frame spacing comes from DYNAMICS_DT below, shared with vdos.py and msd.py
# because all three analyse dynamics.lammpstrj and its dt is one number. The bare
# names dsf.py used to read (DT, N_FRAMES, STRIDE, Q_MAX, ...), and the
# short-lived DSF_DT, now abort with a rename notice rather than being ignored.
#
#   DSF_N_FRAMES       frame_stop: an INDEX into the dump, not a count. Frames
#                      actually used = DSF_N_FRAMES / DSF_STRIDE. BLANK or 0
#                      reads the WHOLE trajectory, matching vdos.py's and
#                      msd.py's "0 = all" — leave it blank and control the cost
#                      with DSF_STRIDE instead.
#   DSF_STRIDE         Read every Nth frame. Skipped frames are still parsed, so
#                      widening the time span is nearly free while computing more
#                      frames is not — set N_FRAMES to the whole trajectory and
#                      raise STRIDE until the frame count is affordable.
#                      For STATIC S(q): stride freely; uncorrelated snapshots are
#                      what you want. For DYNAMIC S(q,w): STRIDE MUST BE 1.
#                      Nyquist is 1/(2*DT*STRIDE) — at DT = 2 fs that is 250 THz
#                      (8340 cm^-1), well above the O-H stretch, but STRIDE = 4
#                      drops it to 62.5 THz and aliases O-H down into the Si-O
#                      band. Example: a 30000-frame dump at 2 fs is 60 ps; with
#                      STRIDE 600 that is 50 frames, 1.2 ps apart.
#   DSF_Q_MAX          Max q in A^-1, static. 20 A^-1 is the range needed to
#                      Fourier transform S(q) to G(r) without bad termination
#                      ripples (ripple period 2*pi/Q_MAX = 0.31 A) and matches
#                      neutron diffraction on vitreous silica. Cost is linear in
#                      N_q x frames: ~10.6M q-vectors at 20 A^-1 on an L = 43 A
#                      cell versus 2.3M at 12.
#   DSF_N_Q_BINS       Radial q-bins after spherical averaging. Keep
#                      <= Q_MAX / (2*pi/L) or the low-q bins come back empty. At
#                      L = 43 A, 2*pi/L = 0.146 A^-1, so 20 / 0.146 = 136 -> 130.
#   DSF_WINDOW_SIZE    Dynamic runs only. Number of time lags; frequency
#                      resolution dnu = 1/(2 x WINDOW_SIZE x DT x STRIDE). Must
#                      cover several periods of the slowest mode: the lowest
#                      accessible q is 2*pi/L = 0.146 A^-1, where a longitudinal
#                      acoustic mode sits near 1.4 THz (~0.7 ps period), so 5-10
#                      periods needs 3.5-7 ps, i.e. ~2000-3500 at DT = 2 fs.
#                      The dynamic path is NOT yet tuned against physics — read
#                      DYNAMIC NOTES in dsf.py, and its memory table, before
#                      turning COMPUTE_DYNAMIC on. The remaining dynamic-only
#                      knobs (WINDOW_STEP, Q_MAX_DYN, N_Q_BINS_DYN,
#                      MAX_Q_POINTS_DYN) are set in distribution_run.sh or
#                      dsf.py directly.
#   DSF_NEUTRON_WEIGHTING  yes (default) | no. "no" drops the neutron-weighted
#                      S(q) columns; required for H/D systems, where dsf.py
#                      refuses to weight at all (protium vs deuterium sign flip).
DSF_N_FRAMES=""
DSF_STRIDE=""
DSF_Q_MAX=""
DSF_N_Q_BINS=""
DSF_WINDOW_SIZE=""
DSF_NEUTRON_WEIGHTING=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared by dsf.py, vdos.py and msd.py
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DYNAMICS_DT is the ONLY parameter the three share: it is the dt of
# dynamics.lammpstrj, a property of the trajectory rather than of any analysis of
# it, so giving each script its own copy would only let them disagree about one
# physical number. Everything else is per script and set independently — VDOS
# needs a SHORT correlation length for frequency resolution, MSD a LONG one to
# reach the diffusive regime, which is why those are not one setting.
#
#   DYNAMICS_DT        REQUIRED when RUN_VDOS=1 or RUN_MSD=1; optional for
#                      dsf.py, which defaults to 2.0 and only uses it on the
#                      dynamic S(q,w) path (static S(q) has no time axis).
#                      fs between dynamics.lammpstrj frames. Sets the Nyquist
#                      limit 1/(2*DT) = 250 THz at 2 fs.
DYNAMICS_DT="2.0"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# vdos.py — vibrational density of states. Reads dynamics.lammpstrj.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   VDOS_N_FRAMES      Max frames to read; 0 = all.
#   VDOS_STRIDE        Read every Nth frame.
#   VDOS_CORR_LENGTH   REQUIRED when RUN_VDOS=1. fs; VACF max lag. Sets the
#                      frequency resolution DIRECTLY: dnu ~ 1/CORR_LENGTH.
#                      2000 fs -> 0.5 THz -> 17 cm^-1, enough to separate the
#                      water librational, bending and stretching bands. Halve it
#                      and peaks blur; double it and you need a correspondingly
#                      longer trajectory to average over.
#   VDOS_CORR_INTERVAL REQUIRED when RUN_VDOS=1. fs between VACF time origins.
#                      Affects the NOISE LEVEL only, never the resolution.
#                      Smaller = more origins averaged = smoother, at linear
#                      cost. 100 fs against a 2000 fs window is a good start.
#   VDOS_MAX_FREQUENCY_EV  REQUIRED when RUN_VDOS=1. Must sit above your highest
#                      real mode or the band is cut off silently. O-H stretch
#                      ~3700 cm^-1 = 0.46 eV, so 0.5 eV (4033 cm^-1) covers
#                      water; dry silica tops out near 1200 cm^-1 = 0.15 eV and
#                      needs only ~0.2 eV.
#   VDOS_NUM_GRIDS     Frequency grid points, default 5000.
#   VDOS_METHOD        vacf_cosine_transform (default, matches msd.cpp) |
#                      fft_periodogram (faster).
#   VDOS_WINDOW        Taper; matches METHOD by default.
#   VDOS_NORMALIZATION phonon (default, integral ~ 3 per atom) | unit_area.
#                      This is the SUM RULE, a different axis from the weighting
#                      below.
#   VDOS_WEIGHTING     Species weighting, SEMICOLON-separated, one total column
#                      each: unity | coherent | incoherent | total. Empty =
#                      "unity", the pre-weighting behaviour. Neutron weightings
#                      weight each element by sigma/m and refuse H/D-bearing
#                      systems. Use "total" to compare against an inelastic
#                      neutron measurement, "unity" for the plain simulated DOS.
VDOS_N_FRAMES=""
VDOS_STRIDE=""
VDOS_CORR_LENGTH="2000"
VDOS_CORR_INTERVAL="100"
VDOS_MAX_FREQUENCY_EV="0.5"
VDOS_NUM_GRIDS=""
VDOS_METHOD=""
VDOS_WINDOW=""
VDOS_NORMALIZATION=""
VDOS_WEIGHTING=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# msd.py — mean square displacement and self-diffusion. Reads dynamics.lammpstrj.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   MSD_N_FRAMES       Max frames to read; 0 = all.
#   MSD_STRIDE         Read every Nth frame.
#   MSD_CORR_LENGTH    REQUIRED when RUN_MSD=1. fs; max time lag, and also the
#                      fit window, so D depends on it. Must be long enough to
#                      REACH the diffusive regime, where the MSD is linear in t
#                      — otherwise the fitted D describes ballistic or caged
#                      motion instead. Check the log-log MSD plot: the slope
#                      must be 1 across the fit window. 5000 fs is a reasonable
#                      start for liquid water at 300 K; cold or viscous systems
#                      need much more.
#   MSD_CORR_INTERVAL  REQUIRED when RUN_MSD=1. fs between reference frames.
#                      Noise level only, like VDOS_CORR_INTERVAL.
#   MSD_FIT_FRACTION   REQUIRED when RUN_MSD=1. Tail fraction used for the D
#                      fit. 0.4 = the last 40% of the lag range, i.e. the most
#                      diffusive part.
MSD_N_FRAMES=""
MSD_STRIDE=""
MSD_CORR_LENGTH="5000"
MSD_CORR_INTERVAL="500"
MSD_FIT_FRACTION="0.4"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# vdos_dynmat.py — VDOS from the LAMMPS dynamical matrix (RUN_VDOS_DYNMAT=1)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one calculation that spans BOTH stages: it appends a minimization +
# dynamical_matrix block to the end of in.input, acting on the configuration the
# MD run finished with, and then diagonalizes the result. The DYNMAT_* keys drive
# LAMMPS (they reach in.input as -var); the VDOS_DYNMAT_* keys drive the .py.
# Leave any empty to keep the default written into the .input file or the
# script's CONFIGURATION block.
#
# Cost before you enable it: 6N force evaluations and a (3N)^2 matrix. At
# replicate 6 6 6 (N = 5184) that is 31104 force evaluations and a 15552x15552
# matrix — ~1.9 GB in memory, ~2.9 GB as text. Budget TIME for the trajectory
# job accordingly, and consider DYNMAT_BINARY="yes".
#
#   DYNMAT_MIN_STYLE       min_style, default cg
#   DYNMAT_MIN_ETOL        default 1.0e-12
#   DYNMAT_MIN_FTOL        default 1.0e-12 — the one that matters. Residual
#                          forces become spurious imaginary modes, so tighten
#                          this before blaming the matrix.
#   DYNMAT_MIN_MAXITER     default 100000
#   DYNMAT_MIN_MAXEVAL     default 1000000
#   DYNMAT_DISPLACEMENT    finite-difference step in A, default 0.0001. Too
#                          large samples anharmonicity; too small loses the
#                          force difference to round-off.
#   DYNMAT_FILE            matrix filename, default dynmat.dat. One value reaches
#                          both stages, so writer and reader cannot disagree.
#   DYNMAT_BINARY          yes = raw float64, no (default) = text. Binary is
#                          roughly half the size and skips the text parse.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_MAX_FREQUENCY  REQUIRED when RUN_VDOS_DYNMAT=1. DOS grid upper
#                          limit, in VDOS_DYNMAT_XUNIT. 200 meV = 1613 cm^-1
#                          covers a-SiO2, whose stretching band tops out near
#                          1200 cm^-1; water needs ~500 meV for the O-H stretch.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_XUNIT      meV (default) | THz | cm-1 | eV. The CSV always
#                          carries all four; this sets the plot axis and the unit
#                          MAX_FREQUENCY and SMEARING are read in.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_BINS       frequency grid points, default 500
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_SMEARING   Gaussian FWHM in XUNIT; 0 (default) = plain histogram
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_MATRIX_STYLE  regular (default) | eskm — must match the
#                          dynamical_matrix style in the .input file, or every
#                          frequency is wrong by a constant factor.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_NORMALIZATION  phonon (default, integral = 3 per atom) |
#                          unit_area. The SUM RULE, not the weighting.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_WEIGHTING  Species weighting, SEMICOLON-separated, one column
#                          each: unity | coherent | incoherent | total. Empty =
#                          "unity", unweighted. Needs VDOS_DYNMAT_PARTIAL=yes and
#                          refuses H/D-bearing systems, exactly like VDOS_WEIGHTING.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_PARTIAL    yes (default) | no — "no" skips the per-element curves
#                          and halves memory and runtime, but rules out weighting.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_ASR        none (default) | simple — acoustic sum rule
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_OUTPUT     output basename, default vdos_dynmat
#
# Mode-character analysis: what kind of motion each band is. Builds a local
# orthonormal frame at every bridging atom (for a-SiO2, an O with exactly two Si
# neighbours) and splits each mode's motion into stretching / bending / rocking,
# the standard band assignment for silica glass. Also emits the participation
# ratio (extended vs localized modes — the boson-peak diagnostic) and the reduced
# DOS g(nu)/nu^2. Adds <date>_vdos_dynmat_modes.csv, one row per mode. Needs the
# coordinates LAMMPS writes right after `minimize`, so a matrix from a run
# predating that write_dump line cannot be analysed this way.
#
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_CHARACTER  yes | no (default no)
#   DYNMAT_REF_TRAJ        minimized coordinates, default dynmat_ref.lammpstrj.
#                          One value reaches both stages, like DYNMAT_FILE.
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_BRIDGE_ELEMENT    the bridging atom, default O
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_NEIGHBOR_ELEMENT  its two neighbours, default Si
#   VDOS_DYNMAT_BOND_CUTOFF       bridge-neighbour max distance in A, default 2.2
DYNMAT_MIN_STYLE=""
DYNMAT_MIN_ETOL=""
DYNMAT_MIN_FTOL=""
DYNMAT_MIN_MAXITER=""
DYNMAT_MIN_MAXEVAL=""
DYNMAT_DISPLACEMENT=""
DYNMAT_FILE=""
DYNMAT_BINARY=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_MAX_FREQUENCY="200"
VDOS_DYNMAT_XUNIT=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_BINS=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_SMEARING=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_MATRIX_STYLE=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_NORMALIZATION=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_WEIGHTING=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_PARTIAL=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_ASR=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_OUTPUT=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_CHARACTER=""
DYNMAT_REF_TRAJ=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_BRIDGE_ELEMENT=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_NEIGHBOR_ELEMENT=""
VDOS_DYNMAT_BOND_CUTOFF=""
